{"question": "A 64-year-old man comes to the physician with a 1-week history of sore mouth. He says that swallowing is not painful. He has had asthma for more than 20 years. His only medication is a high-dose combination salmeterol/fluticasone inhaler. His temperature is 37.1\u00b0C (98.8\u00b0F), pulse is 74/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 125/65 mm Hg. A photograph of his oral mucosa is shown. Indirect mirror examination of the posterior oropharynx, larynx, and hypopharynx shows no abnormalities. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Azithromycin", "B": "Fluconazole", "C": "Isotretinoin", "D": "Nystatin", "E": "Penicillin V"}, "pred_answer": "D", "gold_answer": "D", "question_domains": ["Internal Medicine", "Pulmonology", "Otolaryngology", "Dermatology", "Pharmacology"], "option_domains": ["Infectious Disease", "Pharmacology"], "question_analyses": {"Internal Medicine": "Based on the provided scenario, the 64-year-old man presents with a 1-week history of a sore mouth, with the notable detail that swallowing is not painful. He has a long-standing history of asthma and is on a high-dose combination inhaler (salmeterol/fluticasone). The absence of abnormalities in the posterior oropharynx, larynx, and hypopharynx during examination is also significant. Given these details, the sore mouth could be indicative of several conditions, but the most likely diagnosis in this context is oral candidiasis (thrush). This is particularly plausible considering the patient's use of a high-dose inhaled corticosteroid, which can predispose individuals to oral fungal infections due to immunosuppression and local effects on the oral mucosa. ### Noteworthy Aspects:\n1. **High-Dose Inhaled Corticosteroids**: The use of a high-dose combination inhaler (salmeterol/fluticasone) is a significant risk factor for developing oral candidiasis. Corticosteroids can alter the normal flora of the mouth and suppress local immu