{"question": "A 63-year-old man presents to the physician with a complaint of back pain for the past 2 months. The pain is present throughout the day, and even at night when he lies down. He also complains of difficulty walking upstairs and says he recently started to wear adult diapers because he seems to have difficulty controlling his bowel movements. His vital signs are within normal limits. The neurological examination revealed bilateral lower limb weakness, diminished temperature sensation, and decreased vibratory sense. The rectal examination revealed a hard nodular mass and a weak rectal sphincter tone. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Syringomyelia", "B": "Spinal metastasis", "C": "Herniated disc", "D": "Epidural abscess", "E": "Spinal hematoma"}, "pred_answer": "B", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Neurology", "Urology", "Oncology", "Geriatrics", "Pain Management"], "option_domains": ["Neurology", "Oncology"], "question_analyses": {"Neurology": "The clinical scenario presented describes a 63-year-old man with several concerning symptoms: persistent back pain, difficulty walking upstairs, bowel incontinence, bilateral lower limb weakness, diminished temperature sensation, decreased vibratory sense, and a hard nodular mass found on rectal examination with weak rectal sphincter tone. Based on these symptoms, the most likely diagnosis is a spinal cord compression, potentially due to a malignancy (such as a metastatic tumor) or a primary spinal tumor. The presence of back pain that is persistent and worsens at night, along with neurological deficits (bilateral lower limb weakness and sensory changes), suggests that there is likely an underlying structural issue affecting the spinal cord. The hard nodular mass found on rectal examination raises the suspicion of a possible rectal or pelvic mass that could be compressing the spinal cord or affecting the surrounding structures. Key aspects of this case th