{"question": "A 61-year-old nulliparous woman comes to the physician for a follow-up examination. Her last Pap smear 3 years ago showed atypical squamous cells of undetermined significance. HPV testing was negative at that time. On questioning, she has had fatigue and an increase in abdominal girth despite a 5-kg (11.0-lb) weight loss over the past 6 months. She has gastroesophageal reflux disease and Hashimoto's thyroiditis. Menarche was at the age of 10 years and her last menstrual period was 2 years ago. Current medications include omeprazole and levothyroxine. Abdominal examination shows shifting dullness. There is tenderness to palpation of the right lower quadrant but no guarding or rebound. Bimanual palpation shows a small uterus and a right adnexal mass. Further evaluation of this patient is most likely to show which of the following findings?", "options": {"A": "Proliferation of endometrial glands", "B": "Elevated serum CA-125 level", "C": "Elevated serum beta-hCG level", "D": "Prolonged prothrombin time", "E": "Cervical dysplasia on Pap smear"}, "pred_answer": "B", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Gynecology", "Oncology", "Endocrinology", "Gastroenterology", "Radiology"], "option_domains": ["Oncology", "Gynecology"], "question_analyses": {"Gynecology": "In this scenario, we have a 61-year-old nulliparous woman with a history of atypical squamous cells of undetermined significance (ASC-US) on her last Pap smear, which was 3 years ago, and negative HPV testing at that time. She presents with fatigue, increased abdominal girth despite weight loss, gastroesophageal reflux disease, and Hashimoto's thyroiditis. Notably, she has not had a menstrual period for 2 years, indicating she is postmenopausal. The abdominal examination reveals shifting dullness, which suggests the presence of ascites, and tenderness in the right lower quadrant with a palpable right adnexal mass on bimanual examination. Given her age, symptoms, and findings, there is a significant